---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Core" description="A continuous explanation of the physical objects and approximations that make electronic-structure theory possible." current="core">
  <h1>Core</h1>
  <p class="lede">Core is the Atlas’s continuous account of why electronic-structure problems exist, which quantum objects describe them, and how reformulations, declared approximations, finite representations, and solution procedures make them calculable.</p>
  <p>It is readable on its own. <a href={href('/theory/')}>Foundations</a> supplies prerequisite repair when a mathematical or physical concept needs more depth; <a href={href('/reading/')}>Guided Reading</a> follows the logic of individual sources instead.</p>

  <figure class="core-map" aria-labelledby="core-map-caption">
    <ol class="core-map-spine">
      <li><strong>Matter</strong><span>electrons and nuclei</span></li>
      <li><strong>Quantum many-body problem</strong><span>states, interactions, and observables</span></li>
      <li><strong>Fermionic state space and orbital references</strong><span>antisymmetry, determinants, mean fields, and missing correlation</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="core-map-specialization"><strong>For periodic systems:</strong> translation, Bloch states, bands, filling, and Brillouin-zone objects reshape each route below.</p>
    <div class="core-map-branches">
      <section>
        <h3>A density-based ground-state route</h3>
        <p>Density-functional theory → finite representation and solution → energy, density, force, and structure</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Response route</h3>
        <p>Electronic or force response → force constants; with nuclear masses → harmonic lattice dynamics</p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Excitation route</h3>
        <p>Charged or neutral excitation energies and amplitudes + probe coupling and selection rules → spectra</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="core-map-caption">The first three steps supply shared foundations; periodicity is a specialization of the physical model. The three routes then ask different questions, and response or excitations are not ordinary outputs of a ground-state calculation.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The Core sequence</h2>
  <ol class="core-entries">
    <li><a href={href('/core/orientation/')}><strong>Orientation · What Electronic Structure Explains</strong></a><span>Why electrons connect a quantum state to material behavior.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-i/')}><strong>Part I · The Quantum Problem of Matter</strong></a><span>The electron–nuclear Hamiltonian and the fixed-nuclei electronic problem.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-ii/')}><strong>Part II · Fermions, Mean Fields, and Correlation</strong></a><span>Why orbitals are useful, where exchange comes from, and what one determinant leaves out.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-iii/')}><strong>Part III · Periodic Matter and Electronic States</strong></a><span>How translation symmetry organizes Bloch states, reciprocal-space labels, bands, and occupations.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-iv/')}><strong>Part IV · Density Functional Theory</strong></a><span>Why the ground-state density is sufficient in principle, how Kohn–Sham orbitals make the theory usable, and where approximation and self-consistency enter.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-v/')}><strong>Part V · From Equations to Computation</strong></a><span>How finite representations, core treatment, Brillouin-zone quadrature, and self-consistent solution turn the continuum theory into a calculation.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-vi/')}><strong>Part VI · Ground-State Properties and Structure</strong></a><span>How energy differences, forces, stress, structural optimization, curvature, and thermodynamic potentials support distinct physical claims.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-vii/')}><strong>Part VII · Response and Lattice Dynamics</strong></a><span>How declared perturbations produce response derivatives, force constants, phonon modes, and electron–phonon coupling objects.</span></li>
    <li><a href={href('/core/part-viii/')}><strong>Part VIII · Excitations and Spectra</strong></a><span>How charged quasiparticles, neutral electron–hole states, probe matrix elements, and spectra differ from ground-state Kohn–Sham bands.</span></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where the spine branches</h2>
  <p>The eight Parts establish a shared language, not the end of electronic-structure theory. The next route should be chosen by the physical object that the research question adds:</p>
  <ul class="core-entries">
    <li><strong>Spin, spin–orbit coupling, and magnetism.</strong><span>A spin-polarized calculation searches a different electronic-state branch; spin–orbit coupling changes the Hamiltonian and can change energies, wavefunctions, and selection rules. A small spin–orbit effect on one observable does not justify omitting it for every later observable. Continue with <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Relativistic Electronic Structure, Spin, and Magnetism</a>.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Wannier functions, Berry phases, and topology.</strong><span>When phases, occupied subspaces, polarization, or invariants become the target, raw energy-ordered band labels are no longer enough. Continue with <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a>.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Strong and local correlation.</strong><span>Some problems require an explicitly interacting low-energy subspace or a richer many-body state, even when a self-consistent determinant or density-functional approximation is stable. The model must declare its correlated subspace or Hamiltonian, interaction parameters, solver, and any double-counting convention; tighter numerical settings do not supply these choices. <a href={href('/theory/many-body-physics/')}>Many-Body Physics</a> is the formal starting point.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Many-body correlation and spectroscopy beyond ground-state Kohn–Sham theory.</strong><span>Charged and neutral excitations require Green functions, self-energies, response kernels, or related many-body constructions whose outputs remain distinct from Kohn–Sham bands. Continue with <a href={href('/theory/many-body-perturbation-theory-and-quasiparticles/')}>Many-Body Perturbation Theory and Quasiparticles</a>.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Defects, surfaces, and interfaces.</strong><span>Changing stoichiometry, dimensional boundary, charge state, or reservoir changes the physical system and its reference energies before it changes a computational parameter. <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-chemistry/')}>Solid-State Chemistry</a> supplies the bulk-defect and composition entrance; <a href={href('/theory/surface-and-interface-chemistry/')}>Surface and Interface Chemistry</a> supplies boundary, termination, adsorption, and interface depth.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Electron–phonon coupling, transport, and superconductivity.</strong><span>Part VII defines the coupling object; mobilities, linewidths, pairing kernels, and transition temperatures require additional phase-space integrals and physical assumptions. Use <a href={href('/methods/')}>Methods</a> and source-specific Guided Reading for those branches.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Finite temperature and coupled dynamics.</strong><span>Electronic smearing, electronic temperature, nuclear ensembles, vibrational free energy, and real-time dynamics are different model layers. Continue with <a href={href('/theory/statistical-mechanics/')}>Statistical Mechanics</a> before choosing a finite-temperature or dynamics method.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <p>When understanding becomes an actual calculation, <a href="https://maxwell3919.github.io/DFT-Research-Workflow/">DFT Research Workflow</a> takes over execution, convergence, validation, and evidence.</p>
</BaseLayout>
